  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\n8:00pm Current Conditions Update - Along the Outer Banks,\nnortheast winds are currently gusting up to around 35-50 mph. \nThe highest land-based wind gust measured so far has been 48 \nmph at Oregon Inlet. Elsewhere, gusts have generally been in the\n20-35 mph range. According to the NC DOT, minor ocean overwash\nhas been observed along NC-12 on Pea Island. Elevated water\nlevels have also been noted for areas adjacent to the Pamlico\nSound, including Downeast Carteret County and along the shores\nof the Neuse River.\n\nPREVIOUS DISCUSSION: Forecast is panning out well based on \ncurrent observations, as winds are gusting to 30-40 mph along \nthe Outer Banks this afternoon, decreasing to 15-25 mph inland. \nLow pressure system is sitting about 250 miles east of Cape \nHatteras, with the latest RAP analysis showing a minimum \npressure of 1011 mb. The low is expected to rapidly strengthen \nand slowly shift north in the coming days, bringing a prolonged \nperiod of gusty N/NE winds and dangerous coastal and marine \nconditions. Uncertainty still exists with the eventual strength \nof the low as it shifts off the New England coast as we approach\nthis weekend. This result in continued low to moderate \nconfidence on the end time of hazardous marine conditions and \ncoastal impacts.\n\nRegarding sensible weather, scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms along the coast are expected to persist through \nThursday. Flooding risk remains very low.\n\nBy far, the greatest impacts with this coastal low are expected\nfor the Outer Banks. There, a period of moderate to strong \nnortheast to north winds are developing today, and then \npotentially lasting as Saturday morning, especially if a \nwestward and slower trend holds. Probabilistic guidance shows a \nstrong signal for reaching Wind Advisory criteria with both \nsustained winds and gusts, and the wind advisory for Dare \nCounty, Ocracoke, and Downeast Carteret County remains in effect.\nFor the OBX from Cape Hatteras north, peak gusts could top out \naround 50 mph, and this may lead to some minor tree and power \nline damage, along with a risk of isolated power outages. Roads\nalong the Outer Banks may be dangerous to traverse for high \nprofile vehicles, especially along bridges.\n\nThe strong, and longer duration, of the winds is also expected \nto support an increased risk of coastal impacts. Building seas \nand increasing swell periods will lead to dangerous surf \nconditions in addition to a risk of beach erosion, wave runup, \nocean overwash, and minor to moderate coastal flooding. Wave \nheights in the surf zone may reach 10-15ft at 10-13s from Duck \nto Cape Hatteras, peaking on Thursday. Of note, the highest wave\nenergy appears to be aligned with the evening high tide\nThursday (which happens to be the highest of the two high tides\nThursday). This will likely result in impacts to vulnerable\nportions of the Outer Banks north of Ocracoke Inlet where dune \nstructures are compromised or non-existent. At this point the \n6-8ft+ wave heights in the surf zone may last into this weekend.\nA High Surf Advisory Remains in effect through Saturday \nevening.\n\nFor soundside areas adjacent to the southern Pamlico Sound, and\nthe nearby Neuse River, persistent northeasterly winds may lead\nto minor coastal flooding later today through Friday. Already,\nsome gauges in the southern Pamlico Sound are approaching or\nhave reached minor flood levels. Eventually the winds are \nexpected to turn more northerly, which may then allow the \nsoundside coastal flooding risk to shift to portions of Ocracoke\nand Hatteras Villages.\n\nPlease continue to stay tuned for future updates as this is a\ncomplex and longer-duration coastal low event, with subtle\nchanges in strength and path of the low leading to increased, or\ndecreased, coastal impacts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs the low shifts to New England and eventually weakens, sfc \nhigh and ridging aloft builds into ENC early next week. This \nwill bring lighter winds, sunshine, and warmer weather back to \nthe region. Of note, despite the warming weather, light \nnortherly to variable winds early next week are forecast to keep\ndewpoints in the 50s, making for more comfortable conditions \ndespite highs warming up into the mid to upper 80s.",
